<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/08 § The resource-graph projection, docs/plan/04 § Streams — issue #54's stream and
      projection half. Two things live here and nothing else: the IResourceChangedSink that puts a
      resource-changed event on NATS JetStream under the subject grammar docs/plan/04 names, and the
      projector that consumes the stream on a silo and writes the flattened row into the tenant's
      ClickHouse database.

      ⚠ NATS.Client.JetStream DIRECTLY, AND NOT Microsoft.Orleans.Streaming.NATS, AND THE REASON IS
      MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED. Directory.Packages.props § Orleans pins the Orleans provider at
      $(OrleansVersion)-alpha.1 and says it is a prerelease no project references. Re-checked on
      2026-09-17 against the NuGet flat-container index: fourteen versions published, from
      10.0.0-alpha.1 to 10.3.1-alpha.1, and EVERY ONE carries an `-alpha.1` suffix — there has never
      been a stable release to move to. Three more things decide it once that is known:

        • The producer is the GATEWAY. ResourceManagerService is "a service held by the gateway"
          (docs/plan/08 § The write path, end to end) and the gateway is an Orleans CLIENT
          (docs/plan/03 § Hosts), so an Orleans stream would need the provider composed on the
          client side as well as the silo's, and Orleans.Multitenant's AddMultitenantStreams is an
          ISiloBuilder extension with no client half.
        • The Orleans provider pulls NATS.Net [2.7.2,), a different client family from the
          NATS.Client.Core/.JetStream 3.1.0 pair docs/plan/02 § Data, transport, Kubernetes pins, and
          CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled would then hold two majors of one client in one
          process for an alpha's benefit.
        • What the projection needs from the transport is a DURABLE, REPLAYABLE log with
          at-least-once delivery and per-subject order — which is JetStream itself. The Orleans
          stream abstraction adds pub-sub fan-out to grains, and the consumer here is a hosted
          service, not a grain.

      ADR-005 (docs/plan/02) names "a hand-rolled adapter over NATS.Client.JetStream" as the fallback.
      This is that fallback, and it is smaller than the word adapter suggests: one publish with a
      message id, one durable consumer. If the Orleans provider ever ships without the suffix, the
      subject grammar and the event are already what its multitenant stream id would carry, and the
      sink is the one class that would change.

      ⚠ NO .Contracts PAIR, following CyberCloud.ObjectStorage and CyberCloud.Vault: the seam this
      implements — IResourceChangedSink — lives in CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts, and a
      contracts assembly of its own would be a second place to declare it.

      ⚠ NO ClickHouse CLIENT PACKAGE, DELIBERATELY. ClickHouse speaks HTTP on 8123: a statement is a
      POST body, a result is a FORMAT the query names, and a credential is two headers. The three
      statements this projector needs — a CREATE DATABASE, a CREATE TABLE and an INSERT … FORMAT
      JSONEachRow — do not justify a driver, its version trap, its licence row and its transitive
      pins; and no other assembly holds a ClickHouse connection to share (the Monitor workspace
      publishes its database NAME into a ConfigMap and connects to nothing —
      charts/managed/monitor-workspace/conformance.yaml § owed, clickhouse-ttl-is-published-not-applied).
      ClickHouseClient is a page of HttpClient, the way S3ObjectStore is a page of HMACs.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="NATS.Client.Core"/>
        <PackageReference Include="NATS.Client.JetStream"/>
        <!--
          IGrainFactory.ForTenant — the projector reads the tenant's ICheckGrain for the access column.
          BackgroundService, IConfiguration binding and ILogger arrive through it, as they do for
          every other module: Microsoft.Orleans.Core carries Hosting.Abstractions and the binder.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Core/CyberCloud.Core.csproj"/>
        <!-- IResourceChangedSink and ResourceChangedEvent. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts/CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Contracts.csproj"/>
        <!--
          ICheckGrain.ListRoleAssignmentsAsync, which is what fills the access column — the
          role-assignment view over the parent chain, docs/plan/07 § Azure RBAC, expressed in it.
          ⚠ module-layering.txt carries CyberCloud.ResourceGraph → CyberCloud.Authorization; the
          resource manager's contracts deliberately do NOT reference authorization (their .csproj
          says why), so this edge is this module's own and not inherited.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts/CyberCloud.Authorization.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
